    "knowledge_point": "Solving for Unknowns By Computing a Product and Equating Real and Imaginary Parts",
    "problem": "Given that $-3-2\\text{i}=\\frac{p+\\text{i}q}{2\\text{i}}$, calculate $p$ and $q.$",
    "worked_solution": "First, we multiply both sides of the equation by $2\\text{i}$ to get rid of the fraction and isolate the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3-2\\text{i} & =\\frac{p+\\text{i}q}{2\\text{i}} \\\\ 2\\text{i}(-3-2\\text{i}) & =p+\\text{i}q \\\\ -6\\text{i}-4{\\text{i}}^{2} & =p+\\text{i}q \\\\ 4-6\\text{i} & =p+\\text{i}q\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can equate the real parts and the imaginary parts separately.\n\n- Equating the real parts, we get\n\n$$\n4=p.\n$$\n\n- Equating the imaginary parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6 & =q.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=4$ and $q=-6.$",
